<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Grossmünster</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1100–<a href="/years/1220">1220</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Grossmuenster</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Grossmünster is a Romanesque church in Zürich, by tradition founded on the graves of the city's patron saints <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-and-regula">Felix and Regula</a>, with twin towers that are a landmark of the city. Built mainly between about 1100 and 1220 as a collegiate church, it became the centre of the Swiss <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli">Huldrych Zwingli</a> was its preacher from 1519.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/grossmunster.jpg" alt="Grossmünster" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roland zh · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grossm%C3%BCnster_-_M%C3%BCnsterhof_2014-05-23_12-08-43.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Grossmünster stands near the banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limmat">Limmat</a> in Zurich. Its core was built on the site of a Carolingian church that legend says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> commissioned. According to the legend, his horse fell to its knees over the tombs of Felix, Regula and Exuperantius, the patron saints of Zurich. The story supports the church's claim to seniority over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fraumunster">Fraumünster</a>, which was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a>, Charlemagne's grandson. Archaeological evidence confirms a Roman burial ground at the site. The two churches competed for precedence through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Grossmünster is a Romanesque church and one of the four major churches of the city, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fraumunster">Fraumünster</a>, the Predigerkirche and St. Peterskirche. Building began around 1100 and the church was inaugurated around 1220, using Bollinger sandstone. It has a carved portal with medieval columns whose capitals bear grotesques, and a Romanesque crypt dating from the 11th and 13th centuries. The twin towers were first raised between 1487 and 1492. Their wooden steeples burned in 1763, and the present neo-Gothic tops were completed in 1787.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-wagner">Richard Wagner</a> is known to have mocked the towers as two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> dispensers. Augusto Giacometti added modern stained-glass windows in 1932, and Otto Münch made the bronze doors of the north and south portals in 1935 and 1950.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli">Huldrych Zwingli</a> began the Swiss-German Reformation from his pastoral office here. After he won a series of debates in 1523, the civil authorities sanctioned the break with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>. In 1524 the reformers removed the organ and the religious statuary, and Zwingli and his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-bullinger">Heinrich Bullinger</a> made further changes, among them a ban on church music. Their reforms account for the plain interior. The church holds the graves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-and-regula">Felix and Regula</a>, of Conrad Gessner and of Peter Martyr Vermigli.</p><p>The late 12th-century cloister belonged to the canons' foundation, which was repealed in 1832. The church is owned by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a> of Zurich. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> museum stands in the cloister, and the theological faculty of the University of Zurich has leased the annex since 1976. Both are listed as cultural property of national importance.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Grossmünster” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Carolingian period the town grew around two religious foundations: the <b>Grossmünster</b>, associated with the cult of the martyrs Felix and Regula, and the Fraumünster, a convent founded in 853 by King Louis the German, whose abbess became the nominal lady of the town.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Huldrych Zwingli began preaching through Matthew’s Gospel at the <b>Grossmünster</b> in Zurich in 1519, and from 1523 the city council adopted his reforms; in January 1525 a group of his more radical followers baptised each other as adults, the beginning of the Anabaptist movement.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
